</expression>
</preferred><fmt-preferred><p><semx element="preferred" source="_29408793-23e8-90c7-16ae-07bf930b931a"><strong><semx element="expression/name" source="_7d9a9a4e-f2dc-300a-9231-9d12c40a703b">application schema</semx></strong></semx></p></fmt-preferred>
<definition id="_f3eab17f-20c6-1f30-bb19-b6fe9e6476b3" semx-id="_f3eab17f-20c6-1f30-bb19-b6fe9e6476b3"><verbal-definition semx-id="_375e9810-12ce-1d96-661d-d69d86f23df6" id="_375e9810-12ce-1d96-661d-d69d86f23df6"><p semx-id="_247f7f70-eba1-77f2-5e8b-541eafbff40f" original-id="_247f7f70-eba1-77f2-5e8b-541eafbff40f">A set of <concept><refterm>conceptual schema</refterm><renderterm>conceptual schema</renderterm><xref target="term-conceptual-schema"/></concept> for data required by one or more applications. An application schema contains selected parts of the base schemas presented in the ORM Information Viewpoint. Designers of application schemas may extend or restrict the types defined in the base schemas to define appropriate types for an application domain. Application schemas are information models for a specific information community.</p></verbal-definition></definition><fmt-definition id="_2d0d0268-2bc1-dab2-c84c-d124bbeb56e2"><semx element="definition" source="_f3eab17f-20c6-1f30-bb19-b6fe9e6476b3"><p id="_247f7f70-eba1-77f2-5e8b-541eafbff40f" semx-id="_247f7f70-eba1-77f2-5e8b-541eafbff40f">A set of <concept id="_d39b47da-f9b8-726e-f17a-597f8a03706d"><refterm>conceptual schema</refterm><renderterm>conceptual schema</renderterm><xref target="term-conceptual-schema" id="_f0303a41-3a4f-c898-1c47-2ebd540d2d5c"/></concept><fmt-concept><semx element="concept" source="_d39b47da-f9b8-726e-f17a-597f8a03706d"><em>conceptual schema</em> (<fmt-xref target="term-conceptual-schema"><span class="fmt-element-name">Clause</span> 4<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>1<span class="fmt-autonum-delim">.</span>7</fmt-xref>)</semx></fmt-concept> for data required by one or more applications. An application schema contains selected parts of the base schemas presented in the ORM Information Viewpoint. Designers of application schemas may extend or restrict the types defined in the base schemas to define appropriate types for an application domain. Application schemas are information models for a specific information community.</p></semx></fmt-definition>

 </term>

